WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.170
Ultimately the ability
to learn a language is...

00:00:04.439 --> 00:00:08.150
it still comes down to the three
keys that I referr to so often.

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:10.040
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:15.200
Today I want to talk about the role
of talent in language learning.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:19.790
Uh, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe and click

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:21.290
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:29.385
So if any of you out there have
learned even one or more languages

00:00:29.385 --> 00:00:33.585
to a fairly decent level, you have
undoubtedly had people tell you,

00:00:34.515 --> 00:00:36.585
you have a talent for languages.

00:00:37.335 --> 00:00:40.515
And the people who say this to
you are typically people who

00:00:40.515 --> 00:00:43.395
don't speak other languages.

00:00:43.995 --> 00:00:47.385
Someone who already speaks two or
three languages is unlikely to say

00:00:47.385 --> 00:00:53.125
that because they know that learning
another language takes a lot of effort.

00:00:54.035 --> 00:00:59.970
So the typical person who says you have
a talent for language either hasn't put

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:04.620
a lot of effort into learning a language,
or is stuck with a method of language

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.720
acquisition which isn't very efficient.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.039
They go to the library language, Spanish
class, three times a week or something.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:15.899
Uh, people who are successful at
language learning they typically

00:01:15.899 --> 00:01:19.830
will do a lot of listening and
reading and put a lot of effort.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.600
Uh, I mean, very few people will do what
I do, which is to get up every morning

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.429
and listen to whatever language I'm
learning and listen an hour a day while

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:32.550
working out, while cleaning up, uh, and
do a lot of reading and put in an hour

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.559
or two every day into language learning.

00:01:34.559 --> 00:01:35.429
Few people do that.

00:01:36.089 --> 00:01:41.429
Uh, so typically people who say you
just have a talent for language, uh,

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:45.419
are kind of trying to justify the
fact that they haven't been willing to

00:01:45.419 --> 00:01:49.139
put that degree of effort or to look
for more efficient ways of learning.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.020
But is there a talent
for language learning?

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:57.650
I think it's possible that there
are varying degrees of ability

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.550
to learn another language and a
number of things influence that.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:05.540
First of all, the more languages
you know, the better you

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.610
become at learning languages.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.440
Your brain is more flexible.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.550
You are more open to new
sounds, new structures.

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:15.460
You don't resist the new language.

00:02:15.959 --> 00:02:18.209
Uh, you're more confident
that you can do it.

00:02:18.239 --> 00:02:22.619
You have some ideas of, of methods
that, for language learning,

00:02:22.619 --> 00:02:24.390
that work at least for you.

00:02:24.779 --> 00:02:26.130
So you're a better language learner.

00:02:26.399 --> 00:02:28.679
So, so that's obviously going
to influence, but that's not

00:02:28.679 --> 00:02:31.619
so much talent as, as ability.

00:02:31.619 --> 00:02:34.019
It's not sort of an inherent
talent for language learning.

00:02:34.619 --> 00:02:39.179
Uh, another thing that influences
our ability to learn is the degree to

00:02:39.179 --> 00:02:40.679
which we're exposed to the language.

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:46.645
So if you go to Sweden where
kids television is in English and

00:02:46.665 --> 00:02:50.685
listen to English language, uh,
you know, pop music and so forth.

00:02:50.725 --> 00:02:55.484
They have so much exposure to English
before they start that in Sweden

00:02:55.695 --> 00:02:57.345
just about everybody speaks English.

00:02:57.375 --> 00:02:59.385
So is it that the English...

00:02:59.535 --> 00:03:02.204
that the Swedes have some
gene for language learning?

00:03:02.204 --> 00:03:02.625
I don't think.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:08.640
And it's similar in other places where
people are exposed to a lot of languages

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.570
like in Singapore or apparently in
Ethiopia, although I've never been there.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:17.810
So the amount of exposure you
get influences your ability to

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:22.610
learn a language, but that's not
necessarily the same as talent.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:30.630
Uh, I think ultimately the ability to
learn a language, it still comes down to

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:33.150
the three keys that I referr to so often.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:38.700
The attitude of the learner,
time spent with the language

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:40.590
and the ability to notice.

00:03:41.359 --> 00:03:44.880
And I'll leave a link to a video
that I just did on the subject of

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:46.320
noticing and language acquisition.

00:03:47.565 --> 00:03:49.575
But obviously attitude.

00:03:49.665 --> 00:03:53.745
As I said, someone who's learned a
few languages has a positive attitude.

00:03:53.894 --> 00:03:57.285
They think language learning
is worthwhile doing, worthwhile

00:03:57.285 --> 00:03:58.725
spending a lot of time at.

00:03:58.875 --> 00:04:00.644
They're confident that
they will achieve success.

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:03.840
Typically they like the
language they're learning.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:07.710
So these attitude factors are
much more important than any

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.320
potential differences in talent.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:15.540
Uh, the same with time, you can't learn
languages without putting in the time.

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:19.770
Some people are willing, more
willing to put in time than others.

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:25.800
Uh, noticing is something that comes with
a good attitude and putting in the time.

00:04:26.865 --> 00:04:31.185
So, you know, my point really is
it's a bit like the discussion

00:04:31.575 --> 00:04:33.284
can older people learn languages?

00:04:33.315 --> 00:04:38.145
Like here I am 75, uh, is a 20 year
old a better language than I am?

00:04:38.414 --> 00:04:40.125
No, it doesn't really matter.

00:04:40.724 --> 00:04:41.775
It doesn't really matter.

00:04:41.805 --> 00:04:43.215
We can all learn.

00:04:43.395 --> 00:04:48.015
Even if we assume that some people
are more talented or that a 20 year

00:04:48.015 --> 00:04:52.875
old is better than a 75 year old,
it doesn't mean that a potentially

00:04:52.875 --> 00:04:55.185
less talented person can't learn.

00:04:56.565 --> 00:05:01.695
Theoretically less talented person
has a good attitude, is interested

00:05:01.695 --> 00:05:05.235
in the language, likes the language,
believes that learning the language

00:05:05.235 --> 00:05:08.775
is worthwhile doing, believes
they're going to get there, believes

00:05:08.775 --> 00:05:10.365
in the method they're using.

00:05:11.055 --> 00:05:17.055
Uh, and if that person puts in the time
and then develops, you know, is really

00:05:17.085 --> 00:05:21.960
keen on noticing what's happening in
the language, that person will learn.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:25.830
And if that person is slightly less
talented, talented than someone

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:31.410
else, maybe not as good at imitating
the sounds or, or at remembering

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:34.140
vocabulary, that person will still learn.

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:39.240
Uh, so to tell someone who has
learned three or four languages, oh,

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:41.300
you're just talented in languages.

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:44.690
Um, that's a cop-out.

00:05:46.125 --> 00:05:50.265
That very person who was saying, oh,
you're just talented, like I'm not.

00:05:50.295 --> 00:05:51.045
You are.

00:05:51.435 --> 00:05:58.215
If they were to have a positive attitude,
put in the time, be, be motivated

00:05:58.215 --> 00:06:01.695
to notice what's happening in that
language to accept that language rather

00:06:01.695 --> 00:06:04.140
than resist, that person will learn.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.400
And if you take 10 people, some
will learn better than others.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:10.560
Some will pronounce better than others.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:16.560
Some will, uh, speak better than others,
but those differences are relatively small

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:22.500
compared to the differences that come
from the sort of differences in attitude,

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:27.870
differences in time spent, differences
in methods used for language learning.

00:06:28.469 --> 00:06:30.780
So therefore I would disagree.

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:35.310
Talent as an important
consideration in language learning.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:38.850
Uh, it's something that even
if there are differences, we

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:40.380
can't control those differences.

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:46.650
But what we can control is our attitude,
the time we spend and to some extent

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:50.670
flowing from that, our ability to
notice what's happening in the new

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:55.500
language and therefore to acquire that
language or several more languages.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:59.790
So there you have it, that's my take
on, on talent and language learning.

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:04.950
And I suggest you have a  look at
the video I did on noticing, and

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:07.110
I'm look forward to your comments.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:08.790
Thank you, bye for now.

